            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The state or place of ultimate, eternal judgment and punishment for the wicked, typically understood as eternal conscious torment and complete separation from God.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Matthew 5:22\",\"Matthew 10:28\",\"Mark 9:43-48\",\"Revelation 20:10\",\"Revelation 20:14-15\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Annihilationism or Conditional Immortality, where the fire completely consumes and destroys the wicked, resulting in a cessation of existence rather than eternal torment.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 10:28\",\"Revelation 20:14\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A historical, temporal judgment, specifically referring to the literal Valley of Hinnom outside Jerusalem functioning as a symbol for the impending national destruction of Jerusalem in AD 70.\",\"basis\":[\"Jeremiah 7:31-33\",\"Matthew 23:33\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Universalism or purgatorial purification, where the fire is restorative and refining, eventually leading to the reconciliation of all souls to God.\",\"basis\":[\"Mark 9:49\",\"1 Corinthians 3:15\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":true,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"While eternal conscious torment is the historically dominant consensus and primary interpretation, the exact nature and duration of this judgment are actively debated. Annihilationism has become a common alternative in modern biblical studies. The historical link to the Valley of Hinnom (Ge-Hinnom) is universally recognized as the etymological and symbolic origin of the term.\"}",
